<pdd-reason>Safe explicit file clipboard operations with native macOS metadata preservation.</pdd-reason>
Implement src/file_ops.rs. Expose Action::{Copy, Move}, destination(source,
directory) and perform(action, source, directory) returning io::Result<PathBuf>.
Validate local source names and destination folders. Reject existing targets
including dangling symlinks, same-source pastes, and descendant destinations,
including through aliases. Preflight is not a reservation: publish with Darwin
exclusive descriptor-relative rename so late collisions never overwrite data.
Use narrowly scoped Darwin/POSIX FFI here; no Objective-C, shell, networking or
user-state access. Copies stage privately in the destination volume. Traverse
source directories relative to pinned descriptors, never follow symbolic links,
reject special filesystem items and excessive nesting, and use native fcopyfile
to preserve data, resource forks, extended attributes, ACLs, permissions and
stat metadata. Preserve symbolic links as links, including dangling links. Refuse
publication if the source root identity changed. Normal copy never mutates the
source. Concurrent source edits may cause a visible failure; this is not a
transactional snapshot or a sandbox against hostile ancestor relocation.
Moves within one volume claim the source under a private name, verify identity,
then publish by exclusive rename; don't require content read permission just to
rename. Roll back exclusively on failure. Never let staging cleanup delete a
claimed source. If restoring the original name is blocked by a concurrent item,
retain the source in the private recovery directory and report the actual path.
Cross-volume moves MUST return Unsupported and restore/retain the original,
never use a destructive copy/delete fallback. Cross-volume copies are supported
subject to the destination filesystem's metadata capabilities. Do not imply full
Finder overwrite/merge/keep-both behavior: conflicting items are skipped with a
visible error. Keep synthetic positive and negative tests for files, directories,
symlinks, metadata/resource forks, no-read rename, alias descendants, special
items, root replacement, late collisions, blocked restoration and EXDEV refusal.
